course: everyday
lesson: 242
title: Sharing toys and playing board games fairly
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Games only work when everyone plays by the same rules. Before a new game, read the rules together or ask someone who knows it, then deal fairly, shuffle well and never peek at others' cards.
explain: Sharing is a skill: take turns, use a timer, or find a game you can both play. When a younger child wants to join a game that is too hard, play on their team or pick a simpler game.
explain: Some games are about skill and some about luck. In chess the aim is checkmate, trapping the king, not capturing everything. In a dice game luck decides, so there is nobody to blame when you lose.
explain: Look after games and toys: pack every piece back in the box, roll dice in the lid, bring toys in from the rain, and let an adult change batteries and recycle old ones.
explain: Example: a girl in Melbourne and her brother both want the toy kitchen. They set a timer for ten minutes each, then decide to run a pretend restaurant together.
fact: Let an adult change toy batteries, and put used batteries in battery recycling, not the bin, because they can leak, burn or catch fire.
fact: Use a fidget spinner or small toy in class only if the teacher allows it, because toys can distract you and others.
fact: When a sibling wants the same toy, take turns, use a timer or find a game to play together.
fact: Bring toys in after playing outside, because rain and sun ruin toys and metal parts rust.
fact: When playing pretend with a younger child, let them lead and join in their story; pretend play helps little children learn.
fact: Read the rules together before a new board game, because everyone needs the same rules for the game to be fair.
fact: Count and pack all pieces, cards and dice into the box after a game, so it can be played next time.
fact: In chess, learn how each piece moves; the aim is checkmate, not capturing everything.
fact: In checkers (draughts in the UK), you must jump when you can in standard rules, and a piece reaching the far row is crowned king.
fact: In card games, shuffle well, deal fairly and do not look at others' cards.
fact: Roll dice inside the box lid or a tray, so they do not get lost and nobody argues about the number.
fact: In snakes and ladders, count from the next square, not the one you are on.
fact: In a strategy game, think, but do not keep everyone waiting too long.
fact: In a game of chance, luck decides, not cleverness, so do not blame yourself or others for the result.
exercise: The batteries in your toy car are flat. What do you do with the old ones? | Give them to an adult for battery recycling | Throw them in the kitchen bin with the rest of the rubbish | Bury them deep in the garden | Keep them in your pocket in case they start working again
exercise: Can you play with your fidget spinner during a maths lesson? | Only if the teacher allows it | Yes, any time you start to feel bored | Yes, if you hide it under the desk | No, never, not even with permission
exercise: You and your sister both want the same robot. What is fair? | Take turns with a timer, or play with it together | The older child always gets to play with it first | Whoever grabs it fastest keeps it for the rest of the day | Hide it so nobody can have it
exercise: You played with your bike and toys in the garden. What should you do after? | Bring them inside, so rain and sun do not ruin them | Leave them out, because the rain will clean them | Leave the metal ones out overnight so they can cool down | Cover them with leaves for the night
exercise: Your four-year-old cousin wants to play doctor with you. What is a good idea? | Let her lead the story and join in | Tell her the right way to be a doctor | Take over, because you know more | Say pretend play is too babyish
exercise: You open a new board game with friends. What should you do first? | Read the rules together or ask someone who knows | Start playing and make the rules up as you go along | Let the oldest player decide the rules | Skip the rules and play it like another game you already know
exercise: The game is over. What should you do with the pieces? | Count them and pack them all in the box | Leave them out on the table for next time | Put just the cards back in the box | Let the dog have the dice to play with
exercise: What is the aim in chess? | Checkmate, trapping the other king | Capturing every one of the other pieces | Getting one pawn all the way to the last row | Moving all your pieces to the other side
exercise: In checkers, what happens when a piece reaches the far row? | It is crowned king | It is taken off the board | It goes back to the start | The game ends straight away
exercise: You are dealing the cards for Uno. What is fair? | Shuffle well, deal one at a time and do not peek | Give yourself all the best cards first | Look carefully at each card before you deal it out | Let each player pick the cards they want from the pack
exercise: Where is the best place to roll dice? | Inside the box lid or a tray | Off the edge of the table onto the floor | As high as you can into the air | Onto the floor under the sofa
exercise: In snakes and ladders, you are on square 10 and roll 3. Where do you land? | On square 13, counting from square 11 | On square 12, counting square 10 as one | On square 10, because you start there | On square 3, at the start
exercise: In a strategy game, it is your turn and you are thinking hard. What is polite? | Think, but do not keep the others waiting too long | Take as long as you like, even a whole hour if you need it | Ask someone else to choose your move for you | Move at random to save time
exercise: You lose a dice game three times in a row. What is true? | Luck decided, so there is no one to blame | You must be bad at thinking ahead | The other players must be cheating | The dice do not like you and want you to lose
exercise: Your little brother wants to join your hard card game. What is kind? | Let him be on your team or pick a simpler game | Tell him to go away until he is older | Let him play on his own and lose every single time | Give him the cards that do not count without telling him
test: A boy in Glasgow loses a game of snakes and ladders and blames his sister. What should he understand? | It is a game of chance, so nobody caused it | His sister must have moved the dice when he was not looking | He did not think hard enough about his moves | The board must have been set up the wrong way
test: Why is it important that all players read the rules before a new game? | Everyone needs the same rules for the game to be fair | It makes the game last much longer | So that the winner can say afterwards that the rules were wrong | The box says that you must, by law
test: After a games evening, the dice from the board game are missing. What would have prevented it? | Rolling dice in the box lid and packing up after | Rolling them much harder, right off the table | Using only one dice | Keeping them on the floor under the table between turns
test: Two children at the beach both want the only bucket. Which idea is fairest? | Take turns, or build the castle together | The stronger one takes it and keeps it | The one who shouts the loudest gets it | Throw the bucket in the sea
test: A player in checkers can jump an opponent's piece but does not want to. In standard rules, what is true? | He must jump when he can | He can choose to skip the jump | Jumping is never allowed | He gets an extra turn instead
test: Why should old batteries not go in the normal bin? | They can leak, burn or catch fire and harm the environment | They are worth money at the shop | They can still be used forever if you warm them up a bit | Bins are too small for batteries
test: A beginner in chess captures lots of pieces but loses. What did he forget? | The goal is checkmate, not capturing everything | Pawns are worth the most points | The queen can never be captured by the other player | Chess is won by whoever has more pieces left at the end
test: A boy keeps peeking at his friend's cards. Why is that wrong? | It makes the game unfair and dishonest | It slows the game down a little bit | It is only wrong in proper tournaments with prizes | It is fine as long as he does not win
test: lil bro n me both want the scooter game, he's crying | Take turns, use a timer or play it together | Grab it, you had it first so it's yours | Hide it so nobody has it | Tell him he's too little n go play somewhere else
test: rolled the dice n it went under the sofa again lol | Roll inside the box lid or a tray | Throw it even harder next time | Just guess the number n carry on | Roll it on the floor instead
test: snakes n ladders, i'm on 5 n rolled 3. do i count 5 as one? | No, count from the next square, so you land on 8 | Yes, count 5 as one, so you land on 7 | Move back 3 squares | Start again from square 1, that's the rule
test: my mate reckons in chess u win by taking all the pieces. right? | No, you win by checkmate | Yes, the last piece left wins | Yes, and the queen must go first | You win by reaching the far row
explain: Two brothers in New Zealand fought over a board game; one threw the board, its corner caught the other above the eye, and he needed stitches.
explain: A girl in Texas snatched a toy from a three-year-old, who toppled backwards off a step and banged her head so hard she was sick and had to see a doctor.
fact: Fights over toys and games often end in falls, cuts and bumps to the head, and small children are hurt most easily, so when you get angry, stop, cool down and talk it out or get an adult.
fact: Being a bad loser or a cheat makes others stop wanting to play with you.
test: he took my piece, gonna chuck the board at him | No, stop, cool down and talk it out or get an adult | Yes, he started it, so it's fair | Only throw the dice at him | Throw it at the wall instead so you don't hit him
test: Why is snatching a toy from a toddler more dangerous than it looks? | The toddler can fall over and hit their head | Toddlers always bite back very hard | The toy breaks every time | Toddlers forget it at once
test: Jo flips the board whenever she loses. What will likely happen? | Others stop wanting to play with her | She becomes the best player in the class | Everyone lets her win forever after that | Nothing, because flipping the board is part of games
test: did u hear 2 brothers fought over the same toy and one fell and split his head on the table? real lesson? | Take turns or use a timer; toy fights cause injuries | Grab the toy first so there's no fight | The bigger child should always win | Fights only ever cause hurt feelings, never real injuries
test: apparently some kid cheated at Uno so much that nobody in the whole school will play with him now. what's probably exaggerated? | The whole school; but cheats do lose playmates | That he played Uno | That cheating spoils games | That kids notice cheating
test: my mate says used batteries can just go in the bin, they're dead anyway. right? | No, they can leak or catch fire; use battery recycling | Yes, dead batteries are totally harmless | Yes, if you wrap them up in paper first | Yes, but only the small ones
exam: Identify the reason for which the rules of a new board game are read together before play. | All players need the same rules for the game to be fair | Reading the rules makes the game considerably shorter | The rules change once read | The oldest player alone requires knowledge of the rules
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the aim of chess? | To checkmate the opponent's king | To capture every one of the opponent's pieces | To reach the far side of the board | To keep all one's pieces on the board
exam: Select the response consistent with the established rules of snakes and ladders. | One counts from the square after the one occupied | One counts the square presently occupied as the first | One moves only on even rolls | One moves backwards whenever a six is thrown
exam: Ascertain the correct attitude towards the outcome of a game of chance. | Luck decides, so no person is to be blamed | The loser was careless and is to blame | The winner was the cleverest player | The dice are to be blamed and replaced
